+/// Cross-bound subsumption prune.
+///
+/// `prune_all` (the default path) prunes each `group_{channels}_{bound}.bin`
+/// file in isolation: within one bound group it drops a set `A` iff some
+/// already-kept set `Y` from the *same* group subsumes it (subset of `A`
+/// modulo channel permutation and/or complement). That is sound but leaves
+/// redundancy across bound groups on the table: if `Y` at bound `b'` and `A`
+/// at bound `b` are both present with `Y` subsuming `A`, the subsumption
+/// lemma used throughout this codebase (`C` subset-of `A` mod
+/// permutation/complement implies `bound(A) >= bound(C)`) means `A` is
+/// pointless to keep whenever `bound(Y) >= bound(A)`: any proof step that
+/// could use `A` at value `b` could instead use `Y` at its (at least as
+/// large) value `bound(Y)`.
+///
+/// This function performs that stronger prune across *all* bound groups of a
+/// given channel count at once: it builds a single index over all groups for
+/// that width and, processing candidate sets in ascending `|set|`
+/// (popcount of the packed bitmap) order, drops `A` at bound `b` iff some
+/// already-inserted `Y` (necessarily from a bucket with `|Y| <= |A|`, so a
+/// single ascending pass suffices, exactly as in `prune`) subsumes `A` with
+/// `bound(Y) >= b`. Sets are inserted into the index tagged with their own
+/// group's bound, not `0`, since a *later* (larger) `A'` must be compared
+/// against `Y`'s actual bound, not against a placeholder.
